from research_schema import F

F('kalamkari','Kalamkari: painted and dyed cotton','Kalamkari combines drawing with dye processes on cloth. The corpus uses the term for captioned Coromandel and Golconda textiles; hand drawing, block work and resist stages must be identified separately when evidence permits. Making an image on cotton is not the same operation as painting an opaque miniature on paper.','A floral surface alone cannot authenticate kalamkari, and the term does not specify a single religious subject.','met-textiles met-dyes',aliases='qalamkari|painted cotton')
F('mordant-resist','Mordant and resist dyeing','A mordant helps selected dyes bind to cloth; a resist prevents colour reaching protected areas. Both can participate in a multistage textile process. The distinction concerns how colour is fixed or excluded, not simply whether the finished cloth has red and blue areas.','Mordant and resist are complementary operations, not synonyms for the same chemical action.','met-textiles')
F('block-printing','Block printing on cloth','A prepared block repeats a design on the textile surface. It can work alongside other painting and dye stages. Repetition suggests a useful question about manufacture but does not, by itself, prove the use of a block.','A repeated flower is a design observation; a block-printed technique requires process evidence.','met-textiles')
F('chintz','Chintz and export cotton','Painted and dyed Indian cotton circulated into overseas dress and domestic furnishing. Designs responded to different customers rather than serving one fixed national style. The course connects manufacture, international demand and the later museum lives of these fabrics.','Chintz is not a synonym for every Indian textile or every European-looking floral pattern.','va-textiles')
F('palampore','Palampore: furnishing textile','Museum catalogues identify palampores as large furnishing cloths, including bed coverings. A tree or flowering field is commonly framed by borders in the lecture examples. Function and format should be considered alongside technique and the requirements of a particular market.','Palampore describes a furnishing category; kalamkari describes making practices. One object can be both.','sam-palampore npm-gandaberunda')
F('muslin','Muslin: fine cotton cloth','Fine, translucent cotton muslin circulated from India into European dress and was subsequently imitated. Its delicacy is a property of the fabric and weaving, not a floral printing process. A photograph of a transparent garment cannot by itself establish fibre or workshop.','Muslin is not silk, and transparency is insufficient to assign a specific weaving centre.','va-muslin')
F('textile-circulation','Manufacture, use and collection of textiles','Indian fabrics moved within established Asian and Indian Ocean markets before European companies entered them. Track production place, intended customer, actual use and present collection as distinct stages; an overseas collection address does not identify the original manufacturer.','Made in India and collected in Europe describe different parts of an object history and are compatible.','va-textiles')
F('floral-trade','Flowers as travelling designs','Flowers can be redrawn across cloth, ceramics and other media for different markets. Their resemblance supplies a comparison, while a demonstrated route needs documentary or object evidence. Botanical sources and decorative transformations matter; no fixed meaning is assigned to every blossom in the corpus.','A shared flower is not proof of a direct copy, identical species or unchanged symbolism.','rom-flowers met-vegetal')
F('porcelain','Porcelain and cross-medium comparison','Chinese ceramics formed part of long-distance trade; the Belitung cargo provides a specific early maritime case. A ceramic vessel, a painted depiction of it, and a floral pattern compared with porcelain are three different evidence types. The latter comparison does not identify the body of an uncaptioned dish.','White ground and blue decoration alone do not establish porcelain, Chinese manufacture or a particular trade route.','si-belitung')
F('carpet','Carpets in interiors and trade','Carpets produced in Islamic-world workshops circulated internationally and could be displayed on tables and walls as well as floors. In European interiors they can indicate valued possessions and pictorial texture; their exact place of manufacture needs object-specific evidence.','A table carpet is not an incorrectly placed floor covering, and a painted carpet is not a surviving textile specimen.','met-carpets')
F('bidri','Bidri: contrasting inlay and dark ground','Bidri combines a darkened metal ground with contrasting inlaid decoration. The technique is associated with Deccani production. Its sharp light-on-dark pattern differs from a surface that is only engraved or painted; precise alloy proportions require technical documentation.','An engraved silver-coloured surface is not automatically bidri; distinguish incised lines from inserted metal.','met-deccan')
F('inlay','Inlay versus incision','Inlay inserts a contrasting material into a prepared ground. Bidri provides a documented metalwork example in this course. A visible colour contrast is a starting observation; close inspection or a catalogue is needed to distinguish inserted metal from applied paint or shallow engraving.','Inlay adds a material to prepared recesses; engraving cuts a surface without necessarily inserting another material.','met-deccan')
F('ghalamzani','Ghalamzani / qalamzani in metalwork','Qalamzani names traditions of working metal with engraving and related embossing processes. The lecture uses decorated Iranian vessels to discuss such surface work. A tool-related name shared with another craft does not make the processes materially identical.','Qalamzani metalwork and kalamkari textile work act on different supports and use different procedures.','iranica-qalamzani',aliases='qalamzani|ghalam zani')
F('wootz','Wootz steel and material circulation','Wootz is associated with South Asian crucible-steel production and international circulation of steel and blades. This establishes a material-trade context for the lecture, not a metallurgical identification of a weapon shown only in a painting or relief.','A depicted sword or patterned blade is not sufficient evidence to authenticate wootz.','wootz-study')
F('abri','Abri: marbled effects on paper','Abri refers to marbled paper effects discussed in Deccani painting. In the ascetic image, fluid patterned zones interact with the figures and ground. The museum catalogue supplies a historical technique context; the slide alone cannot reconstruct the exact workshop recipe.','Marbled pattern is not ordinary landscape shading, and a technique name does not settle the image’s spiritual interpretation.','met-deccan-catalogue',aliases='clouded paper|marbling')
F('relief-print','Relief printing','In relief printing the inked raised surface prints while cut-away areas remain unprinted. Woodblock prints are a central example. The image is multiplied through a matrix, unlike a carved architectural relief that remains on its original support.','Relief print and sculptural relief share a word but have different functions and image-making sequences.','va-print')
F('engraving','Engraving and intaglio','In engraving, cut lines in a plate retain ink for transfer to paper. It is an intaglio process, distinct from raised-surface printing. A work can circulate through impressions while its design is adapted into another medium.','Engraving is not the general name for all prints; woodcuts and lithographs use different printing principles.','va-print')
F('etching','Etching and the intaglio family','Etching uses chemical biting to establish recessed printable marks. Drypoint makes incisions directly; the two may be combined with other processes. Cassatt’s colour prints show why naming the final print’s documented technique matters.','An image inspired by Japanese prints can still be an etching rather than a woodblock print.','va-print nga-cassatt')
F('aquatint','Aquatint: printed tone','Aquatint produces tonal areas within an intaglio process. It appears in the documented techniques of Cassatt’s The Letter and the Daniells’ title sheet. Smooth colour or wash-like appearance alone does not establish its use.','A watercolour-like surface does not mean the object is a unique watercolour; consult the print-process record.','nga-cassatt bl-daniell')
F('lithography','Lithography: planographic printing','Lithography prints from a prepared surface through differential acceptance of grease and water rather than raised blocks or engraved recesses. It enabled circulating images and text. The technique’s origin must be separated from the religious or regional subject it depicts.','A South Asian deity in a lithograph identifies the subject, not an indigenous origin for lithographic technology.','va-print')
F('chromolithography','Chromolithography: colour lithographic printing','Colour lithography uses successive colour printings, commonly from separate stones. The Kali/Tara diptych is catalogued as a chromolithograph. Its many colours do not permit the number of stones or printing passes to be counted reliably from the slide.','Chromolithography is not hand-colouring a monochrome print; colour appearance alone cannot distinguish them.','sas-colour met-kali-tara')
F('registration','Registration and misregistration','Registration aligns successive printed elements. Ukiyo-e colour production used alignment guides for its blocks. Misregistration is an offset between intended positions; a coloured contour must be examined before calling it a defect. No accidental misregistration is securely demonstrated in the supplied lecture impressions.','Registration is an alignment operation, not a printing medium; misregistration cannot be inferred simply from visible outlines.','met-ukiyoe',aliases='misregistration|colour alignment')
F('ukiyo-e','Ukiyo-e and collaborative production','Ukiyo-e prints involved designer, carver, printer and publisher, with separate colour blocks and alignment procedures. Their market and subjects are part of the medium’s history. A print attributed to a designer need not have been personally cut and printed by that designer.','Ukiyo-e is not a synonym for all Japanese art or for every European image influenced by Japan.','met-ukiyoe')
F('albumen','Albumen photographs and applied colour','The Beato exhibition record identifies hand-coloured albumen photographs. The photographic image and later colouring are separate operations, potentially involving different makers. This supports attention to the mediation of the represented scene.','A hand-coloured photograph is not a colour photograph made by a modern colour-sensitive process.','getty-beato ludwig-beato')
F('pigment-evidence','Visible colour versus identified pigment','Colour, gilding and material names should be separated. A technical study can combine visual examination with material evidence to investigate a painting’s manufacture. In this corpus, visible blue or gold-coloured areas remain colour descriptions unless a caption or specific source identifies the material.','Blue does not establish lapis lazuli, and gold colour does not establish gold leaf or an overseas supply route.','nature-firangi')
